<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  CriKey MSIX package manifest.

  WHY THIS EXISTS ALONGSIDE THE MSI
  =================================
  The MSI (crikey.wxs) is the route that works on every supported Windows
  without a certificate the user trusts. MSIX is the modern route: it installs
  and uninstalls transactionally, it cannot leave files behind, and it is what
  winget and the Store consume. It is not a superset: an MSIX will not install
  at all unless it is signed by a certificate the machine already trusts, so
  both are maintained rather than one replacing the other.

  IDENTITY
  ========
  Name and Publisher together form the package identity, and Publisher must be
  byte-for-byte the Subject of the signing certificate or the package is
  refused at install time with a misleading "the publisher is invalid" error.
  It is therefore a token substituted by build.ps1 from the certificate the
  operator supplies; there is no default, because a wrong default silently
  produces an uninstallable package. Version is four-part, which MSIX requires
  and Cargo's three-part version is not, so build.ps1 appends the fourth field.

  CAPABILITIES: WHAT IS DECLARED, AND WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT
  ============================================================
  runFullTrust is declared, and it is the only one.

    * runFullTrust: CriKey is a Win32 desktop application, not a UWP app. It
      calls RegisterHotKey, enumerates the Start Menu through IShellItem and
      FOLDERID_AppsFolder, resolves .lnk files over COM, launches with
      ShellExecuteExW, and supervises plugin subprocesses over pipes
      (crates/crikey-platform-windows). None of that is reachable from an
      AppContainer. EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication" below is the
      other half of the same statement.

  Not declared, each for a reason:

    * internetClient: CriKey does open one outbound connection: the package
      manager fetches a plugin archive named by URL. But a full-trust desktop
      application does not run in the AppContainer that this capability gates,
      so declaring it would not be what permits the fetch and would not
      restrict anything if removed. It would be a declaration with no
      enforcement behind it, which is the one thing this project does not do.

    * packageQuery: would be needed to call the PackageManager APIs. Packaged
      application discovery does not use them: it enumerates the
      FOLDERID_AppsFolder shell namespace with IEnumShellItems and launches via
      the `shell:AppsFolder\<AppUserModelID>` moniker, which is the ordinary
      shell namespace and needs nothing.

    * broadFileSystemAccess: would grant the whole user profile. Discovery
      reads two Start Menu directories, and Capability::FileSearch is reported
      Unavailable by the Windows backend because no filesystem indexer exists
      yet. Claiming access to everything to read two folders is not a
      permission model, it is the absence of one.

    * windows.startupTask: would register CriKey to run at sign-in. There is
      no autostart implementation in the tree, no configuration key for it and
      no UI to toggle it, so declaring the extension would put a Startup Apps
      entry in Settings that corresponds to nothing the program does.

  ASSETS
  ======
  MSIX requires the three logo images referenced below; the schema will not
  validate without Properties/Logo, and the Start menu entry will not render
  without the VisualElements pair. No artwork is checked into this repository,
  so build.ps1 requires an assets directory to be supplied for the MSIX route
  and fails naming the exact files and pixel sizes it needs. Referencing images
  that are not in the package produces a package that installs and then shows a
  blank tile, which is worse than refusing to build.
-->
<Package
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10"
    xmlns:uap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10"
    xmlns:uap5="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10/5"
    xmlns:rescap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10/restrictedcapabilities"
    IgnorableNamespaces="uap uap5 rescap">

  <Identity
      Name="CriKey"
      Publisher="__CRIKEY_MSIX_PUBLISHER__"
      Version="__CRIKEY_MSIX_VERSION__"
      ProcessorArchitecture="x64" />

  <Properties>
    <DisplayName>CriKey</DisplayName>
    <PublisherDisplayName>The CriKey Authors</PublisherDisplayName>
    <Description>Keyboard launcher with isolated plugin workers.</Description>
    <Logo>Assets\StoreLogo.png</Logo>
  </Properties>

  <Dependencies>
    <!--
      10.0.17763 (Windows 10 1809) is the floor because it is the first release
      where a full-trust MSIX installs without the Desktop Bridge conversion
      tooling. MaxVersionTested is exactly that: the newest build this package
      has been installed on, and it moves when someone installs it on a newer
      one, not before.
    -->
    <TargetDeviceFamily Name="Windows.Desktop" MinVersion="10.0.17763.0" MaxVersionTested="10.0.22621.0" />
  </Dependencies>

  <Resources>
    <Resource Language="en-us" />
  </Resources>

  <Capabilities>
    <rescap:Capability Name="runFullTrust" />
  </Capabilities>

  <!--
    THE TILE RUNS `crikey-launcher.exe`, NOT `crikey.exe`
    ====================================================
    A tile, a Start menu entry and an execution alias all start the declared
    Executable with no arguments, and the manifest has no key for supplying
    any. `crikey.exe` is the console CLI: with no arguments it prints usage and
    exits, so a tile pointed at it would flash a console window and do nothing.
    `crikey-launcher.exe` is the graphical entry point from the same crate —
    it takes no arguments, starts the launcher directly, and is built with
    `#![windows_subsystem = "windows"]` so no console appears beside it.

    `crikey.exe` is still in the package: the execution alias below is what a
    terminal user gets, and that one is deliberately the CLI.
  -->
  <Applications>
    <Application Id="CriKey" Executable="crikey-launcher.exe" EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication">
      <uap:VisualElements
          DisplayName="CriKey"
          Description="Keyboard launcher with isolated plugin workers."
          BackgroundColor="transparent"
          Square150x150Logo="Assets\Square150x150Logo.png"
          Square44x44Logo="Assets\Square44x44Logo.png">
        <uap:DefaultTile ShortName="CriKey" />
      </uap:VisualElements>
    </Application>

    <!--
      The command line, as a second application entry.

      An execution alias resolves to the Executable of the Application that
      declares it, so the alias cannot live on the tile entry above: it would
      make `crikey` in a terminal start the GUI. It gets its own Application,
      whose Executable is the console binary, and that entry is hidden from
      the Start menu with AppListEntry="none" — one product must not appear
      twice in the app list, and the CLI is not something a user launches by
      clicking.

      Inside an MSIX both the MSI's PATH entry and its App Paths key are
      virtualised away, and an execution alias is the supported replacement.
    -->
    <Application Id="CriKeyCli" Executable="crikey.exe" EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication">
      <uap:VisualElements
          AppListEntry="none"
          DisplayName="CriKey command line"
          Description="CriKey command line."
          BackgroundColor="transparent"
          Square150x150Logo="Assets\Square150x150Logo.png"
          Square44x44Logo="Assets\Square44x44Logo.png" />

      <Extensions>
        <uap5:Extension Category="windows.appExecutionAlias">
          <uap5:AppExecutionAlias>
            <uap5:ExecutionAlias Alias="crikey.exe" />
          </uap5:AppExecutionAlias>
        </uap5:Extension>
      </Extensions>
    </Application>
  </Applications>
</Package>
